    "https://www.lmu.de/en/workspace-for-students/career-services/career-and-networking-events/workshops-and-seminars/business-language-courses/": {
      "title": "Main navigation",
      "description": "Business language courses - LMU Munich Navigation und Service Direkt zu: Content Main navigation Search Main navigation Research Study About LMU DE Switch to German / Zur deutschen Sprachversion Open / close search You are in the following website hierarchy: Home Workspace for Students Career Services Career and networking events Workshops and Seminars You are here: Business language courses Business language courses Skip to content German courses English Courses German courses Tutored Professional Language Course “German for Career Starters” (B2/C1) Dates: April 27 – June 19, 2026 In cooperation with Deutsch-Uni Online (DUO) This is an online course. The content is partly offered in a flexible self-study format , and you will also receive support from a tutor :8 virtual group meetings (thursdays from 6:30 pm, 70 minutes each) 3 individual feedback sessions on submitted work 4 moderated topics in the forum Certificate of completion upon active participation Required language proficiency: B2 Time commitment: approx. 50 total learning hours Deposit: €75 (we will refund the deposit after the course ends if you have successfully completed at least 75% of the course) Topics: Well-prepared for the job interview Successful communication with colleagues Handling large amounts of information Project work Grammar topics: Causal structures, adjective declension, relative clauses, participial attributes, nominal and verbal style Online Self-Study Course: “Starting a Career and Working Life in Germany” Online self-study course from Deutsch-Uni Online (DUO) Duration: approx. 100 hours Course level: B1; suitable for students at levels A2–B2 Free for students at LMU Munich. Registration. English Courses Business English Compact Future dates will be announced here. Johanna Uitz, M.A. LMU's Career Services Consultant for Internationalization & Employability / project manager FIT-LMU International Careers, Events & Seminars, Mentoring, Employer Services Send an email More up Share To share copy Link Share on Facebook Share on Bluesky Share on LinkedIn Related links Home Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München Routing Facebook Bluesky Instagram LinkedIn Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München Geschwister-Scholl-Platz 1 D-80539 München Tel: +49 89 2180-0 Fax: +49 89 2180-2322 Related links Sitemap Privacy policy Imprint Accessibility Press Room LMU Shop LMU München © 2021 What are you looking for? main website all LMU sites More information about Google data transfer in LMU's Privacy Policy. close"
    },
    "https://www.lmu.de/en/workspace-for-students/career-services/career-and-networking-events/q4-internship-flash/": {
      "title": "Main navigation",
      "description": "Q4 Internship Flash - LMU Munich Navigation und Service Direkt zu: Content Main navigation Search Main navigation Research Study About LMU DE Switch to German / Zur deutschen Sprachversion Open / close search You are in the following website hierarchy: Home Workspace for Students Career Services Career and networking events You are here: Q4 Internship Flash Q4 Internship Flash - Case & Casual Friday Complete one or more micro-internships at companies of your choice. At the closing event, you will get to know up to 30 companies in person. There is no better training for your next case interview or assessment centre! Skip to content LMU Q4 Internship Flash on Friday Q for Career Changers on Wednesday LMU Q4 Internship Flash on Friday Enlarge For the past 10 years, we have been offering the Q4 Internship Flash to students at Ludwig Maximilian University of Munich and all other public universities in Munich. As part of an interdisciplinary team, you will complete a one-day micro-internship at one of our partner companies. Over the course of one day, you will work with an interdisciplinary team to solve an exciting business case, working independently while receiving technical guidance from the company. This case is closely based on real-world scenarios or drawn directly from the company’s day-to-day operations. The Q4 has often served as a stepping stone to the next career move for students. At the end of the year, the Q4 series will conclude with a local career event on December 5, 2025, where all teams and companies will meet in person to expand their professional networks. Q4 is a collaboration between the LMU Career Service and the participating companies. You will, of course, receive a certificate for the event. We always take care to ensure—and provide guidance as needed—that students participate in cases that match their fields of study and level of experience. The Q4 is also suitable for international students, as you will coordinate with one another within the team and decide among yourselves which language to use for collaboration. We nevertheless recommend a B2 level of German, ideally C1. Dates The Q4 workshops are usually held on Fridays from 2:00 to 6:00 p.m. during the semester. You can find all the dates on our overview page . Q for Career Changers on Wednesday Business Case Challenge – Seven Days, One Case, Your Solution Would you like to see how strategic consultants approach complex problems? In the Business Case Challenge, you’ll work on a realistic business case—either on your own or with other students. You don't need a degree in business administration or consulting experience for this. What's most important is structured thinking, creativity, good judgment, and a willingness to engage with an open-ended decision-making situation. You have seven days to complete the assignment. Afterward, you'll receive personalized, structured feedback on your solution. What's it about? Companies and organizations often have to make decisions even when not all the information is available. For example, they ask: How can we address the shortage of skilled workers? Where can artificial intelligence be put to good use? Should a new product be launched? How can we reduce costs without compromising quality? What strategy is best suited for a changing market? How can technological and organizational changes be integrated? ... A business case models such a decision-making situation. You analyze the initial situation, work with qualitative and quantitative information, develop options for action, and ultimately formulate a well-reasoned recommendation. There isn't always just one correct solution. What matters is that your line of reasoning is clear and that your recommendation aligns with the information in the case. Who is this format suitable for? The Business Case Challenge is open to bachelor's and master's students from all academic disciplines starting around the third semester. It is also specifically designed for students who have little or no prior experience with business issues, consulting, or case interviews. Students of the humanities, cultural studies, languages, and social sciences bring important skills to the table: They can shift perspectives, make sense of complex information, evaluate arguments, and take different interests into account. These are precisely the skills needed to solve business cases. In-depth knowledge of business administration, mathematics, or computer science is not required. The necessary technical terms and calculations are explained in the case study. How the Business Case Challenge Works In our online briefing, you'll receive a brief introduction to solving business cases and to the case itself. The case describes a fictional company and a realistic strategic challenge. All companies, individuals, and figures are fictional and created for educational purposes. However, the case studies are based on typical issues encountered in the workplace. Working alone or as part of a team You can work on the case on your own or as part of a team of your choice. Feel free to try both options on different dates: On your own , you'll gain a particularly good understanding of how to break down problems and make decisions. As part of a team , you'll see how different academic perspectives can lead to new ideas and better solutions. You can work in a team with students from all departments—you don't have to be in the same degree program. Set aside seven days (about 90 minutes total for the case) You have seven calendar days to complete the assignment. You decide for yourself when and how intensively you work on the case. As a guideline: A total of 90 minutes of actual work time should be sufficient. The focus isn't on speed or perfection. Use this time to: to understand the task clearly, to organize the most important information, to understand some of the calculations, to develop different solutions, to formulate a recommendation, To critically examine her argument. ... Submit Solution You can submit your solution in German or English. Possible formats include, for example: a presentation with up to five slides, a brief solution document, An executive summary with supplementary calculations. Please submit your solution as a PDF. You'll find the upload link in the briefing. Get Personalized Feedback After submission, you will receive a structured evaluation of your solution. The feedback will focus in particular on: Have you identified the key decision-making question? Is your analysis structured in a way that's easy to follow? Did you make good use of the case data? Are the calculations and assumptions plausible? Have you considered different options? Does the analysis result in a recommendation? Do you take into account implementation, risks, and potential counterarguments? What perspectives does your solution bring to light—and which ones might it overlook? The feedback does not include a grade. The point is not to compare your solution with a supposedly \"correct\" model answer. Instead, you'll learn: which it already does quite convincingly, where the argument can be further refined, whether any calculation or interpretation errors have crept in, what alternative perspectives are possible, what your most important next step in learning might be. Can you use D and AI? You decide for yourself whether you want to use AI when editing. For example, AI can help with: to examine a problem structure, To formulate counterhypotheses, to consider additional stakeholder perspectives, to check a calculation, to formulate critical objections to a recommendation. However, the AI shouldn't just solve the case for you. Use it as a partner for thinking and reflection. You remain responsible for: whichever problem definition you choose, what assumptions you make, which numbers you'll use, what options for action you outline, what recommendation you support. What can you take with you? The Business Case Challenge helps you develop skills that are in demand in many job application processes and professional situations: structure complex problems, Distinguish between what is important and what is less important, how to handle incomplete information, combine qualitative and quantitative data, develop creative options, Justify decisions, Present recommendations in a clear and understandable way, Address questions and counterarguments. This format is ideal for preparing for case interviews and assessment centers, as well as for familiarizing yourself with typical tasks from strategy, IT, AI, marketing, and HR projects. When & where? Online, on several Wednesdays each semester, 3:00–4:30 p.m. You can find the dates in our overview . Previous Next Dr.phil. Stephan Pflaum LMU's Career Services Consultant Career Events & Services / Deputy Head of Career Service Send an email Zoom appointment for employers More up Share To share copy Link Share on Facebook Share on Bluesky Share on LinkedIn Related links Home Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München Routing Facebook Bluesky Instagram LinkedIn Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München Geschwister-Scholl-Platz 1 D-80539 München Tel: +49 89 2180-0 Fax: +49 89 2180-2322 Related links Sitemap Privacy policy Imprint Accessibility Press Room LMU Shop LMU München © 2021 What are you looking for? main website all LMU sites More information about Google data transfer in LMU's Privacy Policy. close"
    },
    "https://www.lmu.de/en/workspace-for-students/career-services/career-and-networking-events/meet-greet/": {
      "title": "Main navigation",
      "description": "Meet & Greet - LMU Munich Navigation und Service Direkt zu: Content Main navigation Search Main navigation Research Study About LMU DE Switch to German / Zur deutschen Sprachversion Open / close search You are in the following website hierarchy: Home Workspace for Students Career Services Career and networking events You are here: Meet & Greet LMU Shape Your Career 2026 The “Shape Your Career: Consulting & STEM Edition” event offers you the opportunity to gain comprehensive insight into the fascinating world of consulting and the STEM industry. Spontaneous participation without prior registration is possible. The event is open to students and doctoral candidates from all universities and colleges. Shape Your Career Consulting & MINT - 27.03.2026 Sign Up! Read more Your entry into the world of consulting and the STEM industry This day is specially designed for anyone interested in a career in consulting or the STEM sector who would like to broaden their career prospects. Look forward to a series of inspiring presentations and panel discussions in which experienced consultants share their success stories and valuable tips. Find out which skills and qualifications are particularly sought after in the consulting and STEM industries and how you can best prepare for your career path. The afternoon is all about practical learning: interactive workshops and case study sessions will allow you to deepen your knowledge and familiarize yourself with the challenges and opportunities of everyday consulting work. Take advantage of this opportunity to exchange ideas with industry insiders and make valuable contacts. Venue: Literaturhaus Munich, 3rd floor Participating companies: Altman Solon Basycon CIL Strategy Consultants CYLAD d-fine EY-Parthenon Humatica Iskander Business Partner KPMG L.E.K. Consulting McKinsey Media School Bavaria Medpace Mercer Revolut Roland Berger Weser & Kollegen Hop-on, hop-off. We recommend planning at least two hours for the day. Of course, you should attend the case workshops/keynotes you have registered for. The event language is German. Impressions Previous Next © COPYRIGHT         SABINE JAKOBS © COPYRIGHT         SABINE JAKOBS Dr.phil. Stephan Pflaum LMU's Career Services Consultant Career Events & Services / Deputy Head of Career Service Send an email Zoom appointment for employers More Johanna Uitz, M.A. LMU's Career Services Consultant for Internationalization & Employability / project manager FIT-LMU International Careers, Events & Seminars, Mentoring, Employer Services Send an email More up Share To share copy Link Share on Facebook Share on Bluesky Share on LinkedIn Related links Home Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München Routing Facebook Bluesky Instagram LinkedIn Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München Geschwister-Scholl-Platz 1 D-80539 München Tel: +49 89 2180-0 Fax: +49 89 2180-2322 Related links Sitemap Privacy policy Imprint Accessibility Press Room LMU Shop LMU München © 2021 What are you looking for? main website all LMU sites More information about Google data transfer in LMU's Privacy Policy. close"
